# A05 CDXML to PNG Converter

`cdx2png.py` processes ChemDraw CDXML files, creates PNG images, and reports the files with the lowest and largest calculated molecular weights.

## Requirements

- Git
- Python 3 with pip
- Windows PowerShell
- Open Babel with CDXML input and PNG output support

The instructions below use PowerShell. Copy only the commands, not the
`PS C:\Users\...>` prompt.

## Clone the repository

Replace `YOUR-USERNAME` and `YOUR-REPOSITORY` with the correct GitHub details:

```powershell
git clone [https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git](https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git)
cd YOUR-REPOSITORY
```

If the script is stored in the `A05` subdirectory, enter it:

```powershell
cd A05
```

Run subsequent commands from the directory containing `cdx2png.py`.

## Create a virtual environment

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

If PowerShell blocks activation, you can use the environment directly without
changing your execution policy:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip --version
```

When activation is unavailable, replace `python` in subsequent commands with
`.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe`.

## Install Python packages

Upgrade pip and install Open Babel:

```powershell
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install openbabel
```

Use one Open Babel Python distribution in this environment; do not repeat the
earlier installation of both `openbabel` and `openbabel-wheel`.

If the repository includes a `requirements.txt` file, install its dependencies:

```powershell
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

The complete dependency list must match the imports in `cdx2png.py`.

## Verify Open Babel

Check the Python interpreter and imported Open Babel package:

```powershell
python -c "import sys; from openbabel import openbabel as ob; print(sys.executable); print(ob.__file__); print(ob.OBReleaseVersion())"
```

Check whether the Python bindings support the required formats:

```powershell
python -c "from openbabel import openbabel as ob; c = ob.OBConversion(); print('CDXML input:', c.SetInFormat('cdxml')); print('PNG output:', c.SetOutFormat('png'))"
```

Both format checks should return `True` if the script uses Open Babel's Python
bindings for both reading CDXML and writing PNG.

If the script calls the `obabel` executable, also check:

```powershell
Get-Command obabel -All
obabel -V
obabel -L formats | Select-String -Pattern "cdxml|png"
```

The executable must list both `cdxml` and `png`. If `obabel` is not found, install
a Windows Open Babel binary distribution and ensure its installation directory
is on `PATH`, then reopen PowerShell.

Important: the `obabel` executable and Python bindings can use different
Open Babel installations. A successful command-line format check does not
prove that the Python bindings support the same formats.

## Run the script

Convert one file:

```powershell
python cdx2png.py rx00005.cdxml
```

Process all CDXML files in the current directory:

```powershell
python cdx2png.py "*.cdxml"
```

The wildcard command assumes the script expands file patterns internally,
as demonstrated in the supplied execution log.

## Example output

The supplied successful run produced:

```text
Created: rx00005.png
Created: rx00153.png
Created: rx00249.png
Created: rx00252.png
Created: rx00253.png
Created: rx00254.png
Created: rx00256.png
Created: rx00259.png
Created: rx00260.png

Lowest Mr: rx00153.cdxml (Mr = 87.4845)
Largest Mr: rx00259.cdxml (Mr = 336.2996)
```

Results depend on the input files and the version of the script.

